<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Colorado Party (Uruguay)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1836</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Partido Colorado</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a> Party is one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a>'s two traditional parties, founded in 1836 by supporters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fructuoso-rivera">Fructuoso Rivera</a>. Allied with the liberal and urban interests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a>, it fought the Blancos in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguayan-civil-war">Guerra Grande</a> of 1839–1851 and held power almost continuously from 1865 to 1959, notably under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-batlle-y-ordonez">José Batlle y Ordóñez</a>. Its most recent president was Jorge Batlle, from 2000 to 2005.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/colorado-party-uruguay.jpg" alt="Colorado Party (Uruguay)" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vectorised by Froztbyte · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Colorado_Party_(Uruguay).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
